   NNeettwwoorrkk MMeemmoorryy BBuuffffeerr AAllllooccaattiioonn
     FreeBSD may have a low number of network memory buffers (mbufs) by de‐
     fault.  If your mbuf value is too low, it may cause the driver to fail to
     initialize and/or cause the system to become unresponsive.  You can check
     to see if the system is mbuf-starved by running netstat -m.  Increase the
     number of mbufs by editing the lines below in sysctl.conf(5):

           kern.ipc.nmbclusters
           kern.ipc.nmbjumbop
           kern.ipc.nmbjumbo9
           kern.ipc.nmbjumbo16
           kern.ipc.nmbufs

     The amount of memory that you allocate is system specific, and may re‐
     quire some trial and error.  Also, increasing the following in
     sysctl.conf(5) could help increase network performance:

           kern.ipc.maxsockbuf
           net.inet.tcp.sendspace
           net.inet.tcp.recvspace
           net.inet.udp.maxdgram
           net.inet.udp.recvspace

